"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[634],{634:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"pro-01","slug":"pro-01","trackId":"pro","order":1,"title":"Wyckoff: Accumulation and Distribution","subtitle":"Scenario Analysis Practice with MakeCash Desk","estimatedMinutes":16,"access":"preview","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":17,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-09","HUB-CALC-01"],"markdown":"## Page 3\\nModule 5. Wyckoff. Part 1.\\nModule outline:\\n1. Wyckoff phase abbreviations.\\n2. Accumulation schematic #1.\\n3. Accumulation schematic #2.\\n4. Distribution schematic #1.\\n5. Distribution schematic #2.\\n1. Wyckoff phase abbreviations.\\n• PS (preliminary support / preliminary supply — the preliminary price halt in accumulation or distribution) — the first attempt to stop a trend before consolidation; support or resistance often fails at this stage.\\n• SC/BC (selling/buying climax) — the first sign of buyer interest in accumulation or seller interest in distribution, occurring on increased volume.\\n• AR (automatic rally) — a sharp impulse after a buying/selling climax, usually establishing the trading-range boundaries within which the asset is accumulated or distributed.\\n• ST (secondary test) — testing the strength of buyer or seller intent at the buying climax (SC) or selling climax (BC). After ST, we can say the downtrend or uptrend has stopped and entered consolidation, where a large participant is ready to accumulate/distribute positions.\\n• UA (upthrust action / sign of strength) / SOW (sign of weakness) — does not always appear; a move taking liquidity from AR (the range\'s upper or lower boundary).\\n• STB/UT (secondary test B / upthrust / repeated test) — taking liquidity from SC/BC. Without a local structure break, Spring/UTAD may subsequently appear to collect the final liquidity.\\n\\n## Page 4\\n• Spring/UTAD (spring shaking / upthrust after distribution / the major participant\'s final manipulation) — removes surplus market participants and completes the major participant\'s position building, leading to a consolidation breakout and the true upward or downward move.\\n• Test — testing Spring/UTAD. An opportunity for aggressive entry after a local structure break.\\n• SOS/MSOW (sign of weakness / sign of strength) — price leaving the range, confirming the accumulation/distribution schematic.\\n• LPS (last point of support / last point of supply) / LPSY (last resistance point) — an opportunity for a conservative entry after price leaves consolidation.\\nLPS.\\n• BU (back-up) — an impulse move for final position building before the trend move.\\n\\n[Text From Diagrams and Charts (OCR)]:\\n• Spring/UTAD (spring shaking / upthrust after distribution / the major participant\'s final manipulation) — removes surplus market participants and completes the major participant\'s position building, leading to a consolidation breakout and the true upward or downward move.\\n• Test — testing Spring/UTAD. An opportunity for aggressive entry after a local structure break.\\n• SOS/MSOW (sign of weakness / sign of strength) — price leaving the range, confirming the accumulation/distribution schematic.\\n• LPS (last point of support / last point of supply) / LPSY (last resistance point) — an opportunity for a conservative entry after price leaves consolidation.\\nLPS.\\n• BU (back-up) — an impulse move for final position building before the trend move.\\n\\n## Page 5\\n2. Accumulation schematic #1.\\nstb\\ntest\\nspring\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-1]]\\n\\n>\\n> The lower panel shows a sharp fall on the left, prolonged fluctuations, then sc, ar, st, stb, spring and test. After the spring low, price recovers, breaks upward at sos, returns to bu and rises again. Gray zones appear beneath test and bu. Here, the upper horizontal is 0 and the lower 1, opposite to the upper diagram. A red dashed line passes above the range through an earlier left-hand price area. The candlestick example illustrates the same event sequence without literally reproducing the zigzag geometry. There are no prices, dates, instrument, volumes or timeframe.\\n> **Entry:** Not specified on the page. test, lps/hl and bu/hl label events; orders and entry conditions are not drawn.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. Gray areas are not labeled as stop zones.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. The lower panel\'s red dashed line is not labeled as a target.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** Numerical levels or R:R cannot be derived. Boundary numbering 0/1 differs between panels.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 5 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_05.png).\\n\\n## Page 6\\nstb\\nspring\\ntest\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-2]]\\n\\n>\\n> Several translucent green rectangles overlap above test and subsequent pullbacks. Red rectangles sit beneath them. In the early variant, the lower red area extends below spring; in the later bu variant, the red area is much higher. Gray rectangles highlight pullback bases and are partly covered by color. The green areas\' upper edges coincide with a red horizontal dashed line starting at an earlier left-hand price section. Upward dashed diagonals run through the green areas. Candles on the right rise above the colored blocks\' common top boundary. There is no numerical scale, date, instrument, volume or timeframe.\\n> **Entry:** No textual entry condition is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): green/red block boundaries show possible buys near test and later pullbacks, including bu. The variants\' aggressiveness is not labeled.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** No textual stop rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): lower red-block boundaries show possible invalidation of the respective buys; the early one lies below spring.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** No textual target rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): the common green-block top at the red dashed line shows a graphical target.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** Colored blocks have no Entry, SL or TP labels. Overlap prevents a precise count of individual position tools. Numerical levels and R:R are absent.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 6 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_06.png).\\n\\n## Page 7\\n3. Accumulation schematic #2.\\nbu\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-3]]\\n\\n>\\n> The lower candlestick panel begins with fluctuations and a strong rise to a prior high, from which a red dashed line extends. Price then falls to ps and sc, forms a range with ar and st, pierces its floor at stb and returns through test. A gray zone lies beneath the range. Several overlapping green/red blocks mark possible positions near stb, test and bu. Their green areas end at the common red dashed line. After bu come an sos impulse, fluctuations above the range and another rise above the dashed line; the final right-hand candles pull back. The lower panel\'s upper boundary is 0 and lower boundary 1. Dates, prices and timeframe are absent.\\n> **Entry:** No textual entry condition is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): colored blocks show possible buys near stb, test and bu; separate confirmation for each variant is not described.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** No textual stop rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): red areas beneath entry boundaries show separate risk zones; the early one extends below the stb low.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** No textual target rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): green-block upper edges coincide with the previous high\'s red dashed line.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** No separate Spring label. Position blocks overlap. Boundary numbering 0/1 is opposite between panels; numerical trade parameters are absent.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 7 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_07.png).\\n\\n## Page 8\\n4. Distribution schematic #1.\\nutad\\nbc\\ntest\\nmsos\\nlpsy/lh\\nbu/lh\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-4]]\\n\\n>\\n> The lower panel shows a rise from the left-hand base to bc, a decline to ar and rises at ut and utad. A rectangle above the range extends from utad through test and test 2. bu is labeled between them. After the first test, price plunges below the lower boundary; this low is literally labeled msos. Price then returns to test 2, declines again, fluctuates near the lower boundary and collapses on the right. A red dashed line runs near the earlier left-hand base. Upper and lower horizontals are 1 and 0; hollow blue circles appear at two line endpoints. The candlestick example includes an additional repeated test. There are no prices, dates, volumes or timeframe.\\n> **Entry:** Not specified on the page. test, test 2, bu and lpsy/lh appear, but no separate orders or entry conditions.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. Upper shaded areas are not labeled stops.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. The lower red dashed line in the candlestick example is not labeled a target.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** The upper diagram uses msow, the lower example msos. This source difference is preserved. Numerical levels and trade parameters are absent.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 8 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_08.png).\\n\\n## Page 9\\ntest 2\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-5]]\\n\\n>\\n> A gray-brown rectangle remains beyond utad. Overlapping red blocks occupy the upper area from utad\'s height to near test/bu; green blocks lie below. The red/green shared boundary is below horizontal 1. Green areas\' lower edges coincide with the red dashed line near the earlier left-hand base. A downward dashed diagonal runs through them. Final candles move below both the green area and dashed line. Colored blocks link the upper-return area to the subsequent fall; no separate legend exists. Numerical prices, dates, instrument, volumes and timeframe are not shown.\\n> **Entry:** No textual entry condition is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): the red/green boundary shows a short area near test/bu and the repeated test 2.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** No textual stop rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): red-block upper edges show the risk area at the utad peak\'s height.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** No textual target rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): green-block lower edges at the red dashed line show a target near the earlier left-hand base.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** No Entry, SL or TP labels. Blocks overlap. The lower sweep is labeled msos; it was not replaced with msow. Numerical R:R is unspecified.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 9 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_09.png).\\n\\n## Page 10\\n5. Distribution schematic #2.\\ntest\\nlpsy/lh\\nbu/lh\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-6]]\\n\\n>\\n> The lower panel begins with fluctuations near the base and a sharp rise into the range. bc sits at the upper horizontal and ar at the lower; st and sow follow. The sow sweep below the floor gives way to a strong ut rise above the ceiling. After ut, price falls, returns to test inside a small shaded rectangle, then gradually descends to the floor. Another unlabeled rise on the right is followed by a sharp collapse. A red dashed line runs near the earlier left-hand base; final candles lie below it. Both panels have 1 above and 0 below. Dates, numerical prices, volumes and timeframe are absent.\\n> **Entry:** Not specified on the page. Test and lpsy/lh label events, without orders or a separate confirmation rule.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. The shaded test area is not labeled a stop zone.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. The lower example\'s red dashed line has no target label.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** The peak is ut; no separate utad appears. The candlestick example lacks some upper-diagram labels. Numerical trade parameters are absent.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 10 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_10.png).\\n\\n## Page 11\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-7]]\\n\\n>\\n> A gray-brown rectangle covers the area after ut and the test peak. A red block lies over it, with a green block below. Their shared horizontal boundary runs near the start of the decline after test. The red top is above test but below ut and horizontal 1. The green block ends at the red dashed line near the earlier left-hand base. A downward dashed diagonal joins the entry boundary to the green block\'s lower-right corner. Final candles pass beneath the dashed line and fluctuate near the bottom. No Entry, SL, TP or numerical parameter labels appear. Timeframe, dates, instrument and volumes are absent.\\n> **Entry:** No textual entry condition is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): the red/green boundary shows a short near the decline after test.\\n> **Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n> **Stop / scenario invalidation:** No textual stop rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): the red top shows a possible stop above test but below ut and horizontal 1.\\n> **Target / profit-taking:** No textual target rule is specified. Methodological interpretation (not an original label): the green bottom at the red dashed line shows a target near the earlier left-hand base.\\n> **Interpretation limits:** Colored-block geometry is visible but not labeled in words. A stop beyond ut cannot be inferred: the red top lies below ut. Numerical levels and R:R are absent.\\n> **Visual source:** [Page 11 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_1_RU/page_11.png).\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PART 1.2: THE WYCKOFF METHOD — RE-ACCUMULATION, RE-DISTRIBUTION AND ENTRIES (WYCKOFF PART 2)\\n\\n## Page 3\\nModule 6. Wyckoff. Part 2.\\nModule outline:\\n1. Wyckoff phase abbreviations.\\n2. Re-accumulation schematic.\\n3. Re-distribution schematic.\\n4. Fake distribution.\\n1. Wyckoff phase abbreviations.\\n• PS (preliminary support / preliminary supply — the preliminary price halt in accumulation or distribution) — the first attempt to stop a trend before consolidation; support or resistance often fails at this stage.\\n• SC/BC (selling/buying climax) — the first sign of buyer interest in accumulation or seller interest in distribution, occurring on increased volume.\\n• AR (automatic rally) — a sharp impulse after a buying/selling climax, usually establishing the trading-range boundaries within which the asset is accumulated or distributed.\\n• ST (secondary test) — testing the strength of buyer or seller intent at the buying climax (SC) or selling climax (BC). After ST, we can say the downtrend or uptrend has stopped and entered consolidation, where a large participant is ready to accumulate/distribute positions.\\n• UA (upthrust action / sign of strength) / SOW (sign of weakness) — does not always appear; a move taking liquidity from AR (the range\'s upper or lower boundary).\\n• STB/UT (secondary test B / upthrust / repeated test) — taking liquidity from SC/BC. Without a local structure break, Spring/UTAD may subsequently appear to collect the final liquidity.\\n• Spring/UTAD (spring shaking / upthrust after distribution / the major participant\'s final\\n\\n## Page 4\\nmanipulation) — removes surplus market participants and completes the major participant\'s position building, leading to a consolidation breakout and the true upward or downward move.\\n• Test — testing Spring/UTAD. An opportunity for aggressive entry after a local structure break.\\n• SOS/MSOW (sign of weakness / sign of strength) — price leaving the range, confirming the accumulation/distribution schematic.\\n• LPS (last point of support / last point of supply) / LPSY (last resistance point) — an opportunity for a conservative entry after price leaves consolidation.\\nLPS.\\n• BU (back-up) — an impulse move for final position building before the trend move.\\n\\n[Text From Diagrams and Charts (OCR)]:\\nmanipulation) — removes surplus market participants and completes the major participant\'s position building, leading to a consolidation breakout and the true upward or downward move.\\n• Test — testing Spring/UTAD. An opportunity for aggressive entry after a local structure break.\\n• SOS/MSOW (sign of weakness / sign of strength) — price leaving the range, confirming the accumulation/distribution schematic.\\n• LPS (last point of support / last point of supply) / LPSY (last resistance point) — an opportunity for a conservative entry after price leaves consolidation.\\nLPS.\\n• BU (back-up) — an impulse move for final position building before the trend move.\\n\\n## Page 5\\n2. Re-accumulation schematic.\\nspring\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-8]]\\n\\n## Page 6\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-9]]\\n\\n## Page 7\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-10]]\\n\\n## Page 8\\n3. Re-distribution schematic.\\nutad\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-11]]\\n\\n## Page 9\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-12]]\\n\\n## Page 10\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-13]]\\n\\n## Page 11\\n4. Fake distribution.\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-14]]\\n\\n## Page 12\\nshakeout\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-15]]\\n\\n## Page 13\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-16]]\\n\\n## Page 14\\nshakeout\\n\\n[[blueprint:pro-01-chart-17]]\\n\\n---","blueprints":[{"id":"pro-01-chart-1","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Beneath ‘2. Accumulation schematic #1’ are two panels: a line diagram above and candlestick example below. The upper zigzag first declines through ps to sc. A horizontal labeled 0 starts at sc; the upper horizontal 1 passes through ar. st and ua appear inside the range. stb then pierces the floor, and the following spring makes a deeper low. A broad gray rectangle lies below the range; a smaller one covers spring and test. A short arrow points right from an intermediate peak. After test come lps/hl, an sos breakout above the ceiling, a bu/hl pullback and continued growth.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":122,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-2","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One candlestick panel occupies the page\'s top, with the bottom empty. This enlarges the previous page\'s accumulation example and adds colored rectangles. Price falls sharply on the left, then fluctuates in a range. The upper horizontal runs through ar and is labeled 0 on the right; the lower starts at sc and is labeled 1. st, stb, spring and test follow. Spring makes the sequence\'s deepest low. Between st and spring, a short right-pointing arrow starts at a local peak. After test, price reaches the ceiling, pulls back and then breaks it at sos. bu returns price to the broken-boundary area; another rise follows on the right.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":141,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-3","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Heading ‘3. Accumulation schematic #2.’ Two panels are stacked vertically. The upper line diagram has horizontal 1 above and 0 below. After a decline are ps, sc, an ar rise, st pullback and ua return to the ceiling. Fluctuations then lead to an stb low below sc. No separate spring is labeled. A broad gray rectangle lies below the range; a smaller one covers stb and the following test. A right-pointing arrow marks an intermediate peak before stb. After test, price forms lps/hl, breaks upward at sos, pulls back to bu/hl and continues rising.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":159,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-4","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Beneath ‘4. Distribution schematic #1’ are an upper line diagram and lower candlestick example. The upper zigzag rises through ps to bc. Upper horizontal 1 passes through bc; lower horizontal 0 through ar. After st, price reaches sow slightly below the floor, then returns into the range. ut and a higher utad move above the ceiling. A broad gray-brown rectangle sits above the range; a smaller one covers utad and test. A right-pointing arrow appears between peaks. After test come a decline, lpsy/lh rebound, msow breakdown, bu/lh return and another fall.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":182,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-5","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One candlestick panel occupies the page\'s upper area, with a large white field below. The previous distribution example is repeated with colored position blocks. Price forms a base on the left, rises to bc and falls to ar. The upper horizontal is 1, the lower 0. Later, ut and utad peaks appear above the ceiling. After utad, price falls, returns to test, forms bu, sharply pierces the floor and recovers. The deep lower sweep is literally labeled msos. The second rise, test 2, ends near the first test\'s height. Price then falls to the floor, fluctuates there and drops sharply on the right.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":199,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-6","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Heading ‘5. Distribution schematic #2.’ A line diagram is above a candlestick example. The upper zigzag rises through ps to bc, then falls to ar. Upper horizontal is 1, lower 0. st and sow appear inside the range; sow slightly pierces the floor. Subsequent fluctuations end at ut above the ceiling. There is no utad label. A broad gray-brown rectangle sits above the range; a smaller one covers the decline from ut and test\'s return to the ceiling. A short arrow points right. After test come a decline, lpsy/lh rebound, msow break, bu/lh return below the floor and continued decline.","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":219,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-7","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One unheaded candlestick panel is at the top, with the lower page empty. It repeats page 10 with colored rectangles added. Price first fluctuates near the base on the left, then rises sharply into the range. bc is at the upper horizontal\'s left end, with 1 on the right. The lower horizontal is 0, with ar beside an early low. Next are st, a sow sweep below the floor and a strong rise to ut above the ceiling. After ut, price falls and returns to a lower test peak. A decline, fluctuations near the floor, an unlabeled right-hand rise and sharp collapse follow.","kind":"structure","sourceLine":235,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-8","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Under ‘2. Re-accumulation schematic’ are an upper line diagram and lower candlestick example. The upper diagram has horizontal 1 above and 0 below. On the left, price rises, pulls back into a broad gray area below the floor, then rises to bc on the top line. A fall to ar, rebound to st and several range fluctuations follow. stb is below line 0. After a short rebound with a horizontal right-pointing arrow comes a deeper spring. The next decline, test, is shallower than spring and enters a separate gray rectangle. On the right, the zigzag recovers above line 1. The lower candlestick chart repeats the sequence, showing bc, ar, st, stb, spring, test and boundaries 1/0. A broad gray strip connects the original lower area to the later spring; a smaller rectangle highlights test. After test, price gradually rises inside the range, pulls back and sharply breaks the upper horizontal. Part of the final rise is cropped above. No colored profit/risk areas appear.\\n**Entry:** Not specified on the page. spring and test are labeled without a separate entry level.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. Gray areas are not labeled stops.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. Line 1 marks the depicted range\'s ceiling; no separate trade target is labeled.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No ticker, price/time scales, timeframe or volumes. An additional small label at the candlestick chart\'s middle right is not reliably legible.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 5 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_05.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":362,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-9","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Two candlestick panels are stacked vertically. The upper repeats the previous page: bc at upper horizontal 1, ar at lower 0, then st, stb, spring and test. A broad gray strip runs below the original rise and spring; a smaller gray area highlights test. Two overlapping long-position annotations are added. The narrow one is near stb–spring and the first recovery; its green area extends toward the ceiling, with red below near spring. The wider annotation begins right of test: green spans the lower range to line 1, with red below entry. A dashed line inside the wide green area slopes upward. Price breaks the ceiling on the right. The lower panel is another example without colored trade annotations. After the initial rise come bc, ar and st. Price gradually falls to stb, makes a new low at spring, returns and forms test. Gray zones appear around the lower reversals, with a right-pointing arrow above one rebound. Upward fluctuations, a return to the lower area, rise to resistance and upward breakout follow. Numerical prices and time are absent.\\n**Entry:** The upper panel shows two long levels: below line 0 near the lower reversal, and near line 0 to the right of test. No exact execution trigger is labeled. The lower panel specifies no entry.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Upper-panel red areas lie below their respective entries, near the spring low and beneath test. The lower panel specifies no stop.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Upper-panel green areas end at line 1. The lower panel specifies no trade target.\\n**Interpretation limits:** The annotations are not named aggressive or conservative. No numerical trade parameters, R:R, ticker, timeframe or volumes.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 6 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_06.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":377,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-10","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One candlestick panel fills the top; the lower page is white. It is page 6\'s second example with a colored long annotation. Two long horizontals pass through bc above and ar below. Boundary numbers are not visible here. On the left, price first falls, then rises sharply to bc. After falling to ar, it recovers to st, followed by mainly declining fluctuations. stb and the later spring are below the floor. A rebound produces test beside gray rectangles in the lower area. A short right-pointing arrow sits over an earlier local high. To test\'s right, price first rises into the range, then returns to the lower horizontal. The colored annotation starts around this return. Green extends to bc, with narrow red below entry. Red does not reach the old spring low. An upward dashed line crosses the green area. Price then rises in several waves, fluctuates below resistance, breaks it and continues higher. A small post-breakout pullback appears on the right.\\n**Entry:** The green/red boundary shows a long on the return to the lower range after the first rise right of test. No exact confirmation trigger is labeled.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Red lies below entry and the lower horizontal. Its lower boundary is above the old spring low.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** The green top coincides with the bc horizontal. Subsequent movement continues above it.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe, volumes or numerical R:R. Entry is not named aggressive or conservative.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 7 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_07.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":392,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-11","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Under ‘3. Re-distribution schematic’ are an upper line diagram and lower candlestick example. Upper horizontal is 0, lower 1. On the left, a decline gives way to a rebound into a broad pale pink-gray area, then price falls to sc at the floor. ar reaches the top horizontal; st stays above sc. Subsequent fluctuations gradually bring price to ut above line 0. A pullback with a short right-pointing arrow precedes a higher utad. A fall is followed by test returning near the ceiling. A smaller shaded rectangle partly covers utad and test. On the right, several more fluctuations precede a sharp drop below line 1. The lower candlestick panel reproduces the original upper area, strong fall to sc, ar, st, ut, utad and test. A large shaded rectangle lies above the range, a smaller one around later upper returns. After test, price falls, rebounds within the range, breaks the floor and continues down. Candles at the lower right are partly cropped. No green-profit/red-risk trade areas appear.\\n**Entry:** Not specified on the page. test and the arrow mark structural elements without a separate entry.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. Upper shaded rectangles are not labeled stops.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. Line 1 is the range floor; no separate trade target appears.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No price/time scales, ticker, timeframe or volumes. 0/1 markers are not identified as prices or R:R.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 8 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_08.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":408,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-12","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Two candlestick panels are stacked vertically. The upper repeats page 8\'s redistribution example with a short annotation. Upper horizontal 0 passes through ar, lower 1 through sc. st and ut are inside the range; later utad and test are above the ceiling. A broad pale rectangle remains above the original range, with a smaller shaded area near the late return. The colored annotation begins right of utad near test: red lies above entry, while green extends downward well below line 1. A downward dashed line crosses it. Price first declines within the range, rebounds toward the top, then sharply breaks sc and reaches the green area\'s lower part. The lower panel shows another example without trade annotations. After falling to sc, price forms ar at line 0 and st within the range, followed by many sideways fluctuations. On the right, ut exceeds resistance; after a deep fall, price returns to test in a shaded upper rectangle. A sharp fall then crosses lower line 1, ending in lower consolidation with a small rebound.\\n**Entry:** The upper red/green boundary shows a short near the test return, slightly below line 0. The lower panel specifies no entry.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Upper red lies above entry and covers test\'s upper area. The lower panel specifies no stop.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Upper green ends below line 1 and sc. The lower panel specifies no trade target.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No numerical trade parameters, ticker, timeframe, time or volumes. The lower panel has ut and test but no separate utad label.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 9 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_09.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":423,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-13","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One candlestick panel is at the top; most of the remaining page is white. The previous page\'s lower example is repeated with a short annotation. Upper horizontal 0 runs through ar; lower 1 through sc. On the left, price falls from the initial high to sc, then returns to ar. st\'s subsequent decline does not reach sc. Fluctuations fill the middle of the range. Near the right edge, ut briefly exceeds the upper horizontal. After declining into the lower range, price recovers sharply to test. A pale rectangle covers ut and test near the ceiling. The trade annotation begins on the final recovery. Red is above, its top above line 0 and the test peak. Its boundary with green is noticeably below line 0. Green extends through line 1 to the subsequent low, with a downward dashed line inside. After test, price falls sharply with intermediate rebounds, breaks sc and reaches the highlighted area\'s lower part. On the right, recovery and narrow fluctuations occur below the old range.\\n**Entry:** The red/green boundary shows a short below line 0 after the test return. Order-placement sequence is not labeled.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Red\'s upper edge is above line 0 and the depicted test peak.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Green\'s lower edge is below line 1, near the later decline\'s low.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe, volumes or numerical R:R. Colored annotations do not specify a separate textual confirmation rule.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 10 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_10.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":438,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-14","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Heading ‘4. Fake distribution.’ Two panels show distribution-like structure followed by growth. The upper panel is a zigzag between two horizontals. After an initial rise and pullback into a lower gray area, price reaches bc at the ceiling, falls to ar at the floor and recovers to st. Fluctuations continue; ut and a higher utad exceed resistance. After these upper sweeps, price falls to the floor, rebounds several times and pierces it. A broad gray strip lies beneath the range, with a smaller gray rectangle highlighting a late return. Two short arrows point right at different structural sections. On the right, the zigzag rises sharply above the ceiling. The lower candlestick example has upper boundary 1 and lower 0. bc, ar, st and ut are labeled; no separate utad appears. After ut, price gradually declines and forms narrow fluctuations near the floor, with a short arrow marking their local top. Red dashed segments mark lower lows, with a gray strip below support. A late penetration of this strip is followed by strong growth across the range and above line 1.\\n**Entry:** Not specified on the page. Arrows and lower shaded areas have no order notation.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Not specified on the page. Red dashed segments are not labeled stops.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Not specified on the page. Price exceeds resistance without a separately highlighted trade target.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe or volumes. Late lower sweeps are not labeled spring or shakeout on this page.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 11 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_11.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":453,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-15","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Two candlestick panels continue Fake distribution. The upper repeats page 11\'s lower example with a long annotation. Upper horizontal 1 passes through bc, lower 0 through ar. st is inside the range, ut above the ceiling. After ut, price falls into the lower range, forms small consolidation beneath a short arrow and makes a late penetration of the gray area below support. Red dashed segments mark lower levels. The colored annotation begins around the recovery after this penetration. Entry is slightly below line 0, with red below and green rising slightly above line 1. An upward dashed line runs through green; actual growth continues above it. The lower panel shows another example without colored trade annotations. bc, ar, st and ut appear. After the upper sweep, price fluctuates for a long time and gradually declines. On the right, a long lower wick pierces line 0 and reaches a narrow gray strip; ‘shakeout’ is labeled below. A return is followed by sharp growth above line 1, then a pullback after a new high.\\n**Entry:** The upper colored boundary shows a long around recovery from the lower gray zone, slightly below line 0. The lower panel specifies no entry.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Upper red ends beneath the late lower sweep. The lower panel specifies no stop.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Upper green ends slightly above line 1. The lower panel specifies no trade target.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe, volumes or numerical R:R. Only the lower panel has a literal shakeout label.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 12 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_12.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":468,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-16","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"Two candlestick panels appear. The upper repeats page 12\'s lower example with a long annotation. Horizontal 1 passes through bc, horizontal 0 through ar. st and a high ut above resistance are visible. After prolonged fluctuations, price falls to the floor and forms shakeout with a long lower wick. A thin gray strip remains beneath support. A short arrow points right near the initial recovery. Colored annotations begin right of shakeout: entry is already within the range, noticeably above line 0. Red extends downward through support toward the lower sweep; green rises substantially above line 1 with an upward dashed line inside. Price breaks resistance, reaches the upper area and pulls back. The lower panel is another example without colored trade annotations. bc, ar, st, ut, shakeout and horizontals 1/0 appear. Sideways movement follows ut. Later, price falls sharply, pierces support with a long wick and returns, with a short arrow near the return. A sustained rise then breaks the ceiling. After a high peak comes a deep correction to and slightly below former resistance.\\n**Entry:** The upper colored boundary shows a long on recovery after shakeout, inside the range above line 0. The lower panel specifies no entry.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Upper red extends below line 0 toward the shakeout extreme. The lower panel specifies no stop.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Upper green ends well above line 1. The lower panel specifies no trade target.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe, volumes or numerical R:R. A later correction is shown, but no position-management rule.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 13 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_13.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":483,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."},{"id":"pro-01-chart-17","title":"MakeCash Chart Blueprint","description":"One candlestick panel occupies the page\'s upper area. It repeats page 13\'s lower example with a colored long annotation. Upper horizontal 1 starts near bc, lower 0 passes through ar. On the left, an uptrend becomes a range: bc is followed by ar, recovery to st and further fluctuations. In the middle, high ut pierces resistance with a long upper wick. Price returns and continues sideways, then falls and forms shakeout. Its long lower wick extends below line 0, with the label below the low. A short right-pointing arrow marks the initial recovery. The colored rectangle starts slightly right of the sweep. Its green/red boundary lies inside the range above line 0. Red extends below support and the shakeout\'s lower wick. Green rises well above line 1, with an upward dashed line inside. Price rises from entry, breaks resistance and peaks slightly above the green rectangle. A substantial correction follows. Final right-hand candles return to and slightly below the former ceiling. No other panels or explanatory text appear.\\n**Entry:** The green/red boundary shows a long on recovery after shakeout, inside the range above line 0. No exact execution condition is labeled.\\n**Entry context from the original text (p. 4):** On page 4, Test after Spring/UTAD is associated with aggressive entry after a local structure break; LPS/LPSY after leaving consolidation is associated with conservative entry. This is the author\'s general explanation of events, not proof that both variants or an exact order are drawn on every chart. This definition specifies neither stop nor target.\\n**Stop / scenario invalidation:** Red\'s lower boundary is below line 0 and the shakeout extreme.\\n**Target / profit-taking:** Green\'s upper boundary is well above line 1, near the later high. Price briefly exceeds it.\\n**Interpretation limits:** No prices, time, ticker, timeframe, volumes or numerical R:R. Profit-taking, stop adjustment and correction-management rules are unspecified.\\n**Visual source:** [Page 14 PNG](../books_pdf_pages/Wyckoff_2_RU/page_14.png).","kind":"wyckoff","sourceLine":497,"limits":"Educational reconstruction from the description. Prices, ticker and dates are not confirmed by the original image."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q-pro-01-1","prompt":"What is the key difference between aggressive Test entry after Spring and conservative LPS entry in Wyckoff accumulation?","options":[{"id":"pro-01-1-1","label":"Test entry occurs inside the range after a local structure break (Shift/CHoCH), with a stop beyond Spring; LPS entry follows a confirmed breakout from the range (SOS)","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Test entry occurs inside the range after a local structure break (Shift/CHoCH), with a stop beyond Spring; LPS entry follows a confirmed breakout from the range (SOS)"},{"id":"pro-01-1-2","label":"LPS is always below the Spring low","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Test entry occurs inside the range after a local structure break (Shift/CHoCH), with a stop beyond Spring; LPS entry follows a confirmed breakout from the range (SOS)"},{"id":"pro-01-1-3","label":"The Wyckoff method does not use stop-losses","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Test entry occurs inside the range after a local structure break (Shift/CHoCH), with a stop beyond Spring; LPS entry follows a confirmed breakout from the range (SOS)"}],"explanation":"Test entry occurs inside the range after a local structure break (Shift/CHoCH), with a stop beyond Spring; LPS entry follows a confirmed breakout from the range (SOS)","sourceRef":"SMC_PRO:71–112, 121–150"},{"id":"q-pro-01-2","prompt":"What is the function of Spring in accumulation and UTAD in distribution?","options":[{"id":"pro-01-2-1","label":"Removing surplus retail participants beyond the range boundary (SC/BC) and collecting final liquidity for a major participant before the true consolidation breakout","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Removing surplus retail participants beyond the range boundary (SC/BC) and collecting final liquidity for a major participant before the true consolidation breakout"},{"id":"pro-01-2-2","label":"Permanently halting trading in the asset","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Removing surplus retail participants beyond the range boundary (SC/BC) and collecting final liquidity for a major participant before the true consolidation breakout"},{"id":"pro-01-2-3","label":"Distributing dividends evenly","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Removing surplus retail participants beyond the range boundary (SC/BC) and collecting final liquidity for a major participant before the true consolidation breakout"}],"explanation":"Removing surplus retail participants beyond the range boundary (SC/BC) and collecting final liquidity for a major participant before the true consolidation breakout","sourceRef":"SMC_PRO:71–81, 313–325"},{"id":"q-pro-01-3","prompt":"How does Fake Distribution differ from ordinary distribution in Wyckoff Part 2?","options":[{"id":"pro-01-3-1","label":"After an upper sweep (UT/UTAD) and decline to the floor, a final support penetration (Shakeout) is followed by impulsive growth above BC","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. After an upper sweep (UT/UTAD) and decline to the floor, a final support penetration (Shakeout) is followed by impulsive growth above BC"},{"id":"pro-01-3-2","label":"Price falls to zero without pullbacks","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. After an upper sweep (UT/UTAD) and decline to the floor, a final support penetration (Shakeout) is followed by impulsive growth above BC"},{"id":"pro-01-3-3","label":"It has no upper range boundary","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. After an upper sweep (UT/UTAD) and decline to the floor, a final support penetration (Shakeout) is followed by impulsive growth above BC"}],"explanation":"After an upper sweep (UT/UTAD) and decline to the floor, a final support penetration (Shakeout) is followed by impulsive growth above BC","sourceRef":"SMC_PRO:448–504"}],"source":{"file":"02_TRIPWIRE_MakeCash_Institutional_Wyckoff_PO3_OrderFlow_Playbook.md","sha256":"4abe2acf380699b03795749b83374881dc7eff04cc6a0902396214d1d1ef96b3","lineStart":7,"lineEnd":511},"isPreview":true}')}}]);